{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310743,"nonce":"Sim73I8aPnuwc+MrqfgeEFvxaCY=","title":"RESOLVED 2026-06-05. W0020 \\\"Argument IrId(N) not found in value_map\\\" trap-stub installs — root cause was InliningPass not syncing ...","message":"Single-line fix at [compiler/src/ir/inlining.rs:676](compiler/src/ir/inlining.rs#L676):\n\n```rust\ncaller.next_reg_id = caller.next_reg_id.max(*next_reg_id);\n```\n\nInserted at the end of `inline_call_site`, just before the final `Ok(())`. Syncs the caller function's per-function `next_reg_id` with the global counter that `inline_call_site` advances for new register allocations.\n\n\n`InliningPass::run_on_module` keeps a local `next_reg_id: u32` counter (initialized to `max(existing dest IrIds) + 1` across the whole module). `inline_call_site` allocates new caller-side IrIds for the inlined body via `IrId::new(*next_reg_id); *next_reg_id += 1;`. But it NEVER wrote that counter back to `caller.next_reg_id`.\n\nResult: after inlining, `caller.next_reg_id` was stale — pointing at where hir_to_mir had left it, which was BEFORE the inlined body's allocations. Subsequent optimization passes that called `caller.alloc_reg()` (notably BoundsCheckElimination) got back IrIds that were ALREADY USED by inlined instructions.\n\nThat produced **duplicate SSA defs** — two different instructions both writing to the same IrId. The MIR-level SSA invariant was broken silently.\n\nThe downstream chain that made this manifest as a runtime hang rather than a panic:\n1. **GVN** value-numbers `Cast { src, to_ty }` instructions by their `(src, to_ty)` key. With two `Cast { dest: 86, src: 36, to_ty: I64 }` and `Cast { dest: 90, src: 36, to_ty: I64 }` defs in the same function (one from inlining, one from BCE's `replace_with_inline_access`), GVN correctly identifies them as the same value and substitutes `86 → 90`.\n2. The Copy emitted by the inliner — `Copy { dest: 62, src: 86 }` — becomes `Copy { dest: 62, src: 90 }` after GVN substitution.\n3. IrId(90) is defined in a completely different block (the first loop body), not on the dominance path to block 16 (the second loop's iterator setup).\n4. Cranelift's value_map lookup at codegen fails: \"Source value IrId(90) not found.\"\n5. Cranelift backend silently installs a trap stub for `Main.main` (per `bugs_trap_stub_cascade`).\n6. In small programs the trap stub returns no-op; in nue/llama-chat where main runs to `[gen] streaming...` before any trap-stubbed callee is dispatched, this manifests as a silent hang.\n\n\nThe bug was shape-dependent:\n- Needed a function with ≥ 2 instances of a value-number-equivalent expression (typically `Cast(I32 → I64)` appearing in both an inlined body and BCE's `replace_with_inline_access` for array index lowering).\n- Needed BCE to run AFTER inlining and allocate fresh regs that happened to collide with already-used inlined regs.\n- The collision was deterministic given source shape but variable across small source edits — adding fields, changing loop counts, etc. shifted the IrId allocation order and the bug fired or didn't.\n\nThat matches the prior memory's framing of \"fields shift class layout, surfacing latent JIT bugs in large import graphs\" — but the layout shift itself wasn't the bug. The shift just changed which functions got chosen for inlining and which BCE call sites got rewritten, which changed whether two `Cast(I32 → I64)` instructions happened to end up in the same function with colliding IrIds.\n\n\n```haxe\nclass Seven {\n    public var maxSeqLen:Int; public var tag:Int;\n    public function new(maxSeqLen:Int, tag:Int) {\n        this.maxSeqLen = maxSeqLen; this.tag = tag;\n    }\n}\nclass Main {\n    static public function main():Void {\n        var arr:Array\u003cSeven\u003e = [];\n        for (i in 0...4) arr.push(new Seven(2048, i));\n        var acc = 0;\n        for (i in 0...4) acc += arr[i].tag + arr[i].tag;   // 2 indexed reads\n        var sum = 0;\n        for (n in arr) sum += n.tag;                       // for-in MUST be here\n        Sys.println(\"sum=\" + sum);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nBefore fix: `Warning: [W0020] ... reason: Source value IrId(90) not found`. After fix: `sum=6`.\n\n\n- **Minimal repro:** PASS (`sum=6`).\n- **Larger repro** (16 layers, per-layer Sys.println, sum-reduce): PASS (`loop sum=34040`).\n- **128/128 haxe regression suite:** PASS — no regressions.\n- **Paris MATCH** on Llama 3.2 1B Q4_K_M canonical \"What is the capital of France?\": \"The capital of France is Paris.\" unchanged.\n- **Decode perf, paired A/B (N=10 each, alternating)** on Paris-history 64-tok workload:\n  - Baseline median: 14.03 tok/s\n  - Post-fix median: 17.84 tok/s\n  - **+27% on short workload**\n- **Long-form perf** (user-reported, 964-token Voronoi prompt):\n  - Pre-fix: 18.14 tok/s\n  - Post-fix: 31.14 tok/s\n  - **+71.7% — restores historical baseline**\n\nThe bigger long-form delta makes sense: the previously trap-stubbed function paths were hit MORE often per token on longer generations (more iteration over arrays, more cross-loop value propagation). When the bug fired, downstream code did expensive runtime fallbacks; with the fix, the optimizer's BCE + GVN actually work end-to-end and the per-token decode cost drops substantially.\n\n\n- Symptom: `W0020 cannot compile` with reason `Argument IrId(N) not found in value_map` or `Source value IrId(N) not found`.\n- Trap-stub install via `bugs_trap_stub_cascade` then SIGILL or silent hang at the next call to the affected function.\n- Repro is shape-dependent and often involves arrays + multiple for-loops + array index access patterns.\n- Triggered or worsened by any source change that shifts IrId allocation order (field additions, loop count changes, new local variables).\n\nIf anything matches this shape, the first check should be: are any two instructions in the same function writing to the same IrId? Grep the pre-codegen MIR (via `RAYZOR_DUMP_VALUE_MAP_MISS=1` in dev — diagnostic was removed post-fix but can be re-added from the bug memory archive) for duplicate `dest: IrId(N)` patterns within one function.\n\n\n- Q8_0 KV cache wiring: the previously-documented \"Sys.getEnv-in-ctor decode hang\" framing was wrong — the hang was the same trap-stub install caused by this SSA bug. Q8 KV can now be retried via the opaque-extern-class pattern + nue-plugins ABI without hitting silent hangs from incidental layout shifts.\n- Any future feature work that adds class fields, loop variants, or method calls in nue's hot path can land without \"fragile to lowering shape\" caveats.\n\n\n- [[bugs-q8-kv-cache-attempted]] — original framing as Sys.getEnv-in-ctor decode hang. Same bug, wrong root-cause attribution. Update next time we touch that memory.\n- [[bugs-trap-stub-cascade]] — explains the silent-hang mechanism downstream of W0020.\n- [[feedback-no-silent-dispatch-fallthrough]] — same principle: when SSA invariants break, ERROR; never silently install a trap stub. The cranelift backend's W0020 → trap-stub path should arguably be hardened to refuse to install a stub for `main` specifically, since silent main-stubs produce the worst possible UX.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_w0020_value_map_miss_cross_loop` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}